{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The argument commits a straw man fallacy by asserting that Popper's rejection of induction is itself an inductive generalization ('a universal claim that cannot be corroborated without using the very inductive practices it forbids'). Popper's position is not that induction has failed in the past and therefore will fail in the future (which would be inductive), but that the logical structure of induction is invalid regardless of empirical history. The problem asks whether the *preference* for better-corroborated theories smuggles in induction; the target fails to address this specific pragmatic dilemma, instead attacking a non-existent circularity in the foundational rejection of induction. By conflating the logical critique of induction with an empirical claim about its failure, the target misses the core tension: how to justify action (preference) without probability, which is the actual 'smuggling' charge the prompt requires addressing.","counterexample":["Popper argues that even if every observed swan is white, the statement 'all swans are white' does not logically follow; this is a deductive point about non-sequitur, not an inductive prediction that black swans will appear."],"premise":"The target mischaracterizes Popper's rejection of induction as a universal claim requiring inductive justification, whereas Popper explicitly frames it as a deductive critique of the logical validity of inductive inference.","successor_question":"If the rejection of induction is purely deductive, on what non-probabilistic grounds does Popper justify the rational preference for a theory that has survived severe tests over one that has not?","target":"63a7e5c46ebdfec503417d52959e61ee7b1c13e30769cdfb7559b0f58f48561d"}]}